1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #Thank you for sharing these PIO programs. They cover an interesting mix of video, audio, and
1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #peripheral interfaces—clearly a system built around a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 (or similar) generating
1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #composite video and related signals. I’ll comment on each file, focusing on what seems new or
1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #different compared to typical PIO examples, and note any technical points worth discussing.
1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #• chroma.pio – PAL colour subcarrier generation with a 2‑pin DAC is unusual and
1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #clever. Typical PIO video projects output only luminance (CVBS) or use a resistor DAC for
1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #greyscale. Encoding quadrature‑modulated chrominance at 4× f_sc directly with PIO cycles (4 cycles
1dae6e3d [deepseek-v4] #per 90° phase) is an elegant low‑overhead approach. The use of a FIFO word to control burst width
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #and the irq handshake with the main video state machine (“vidbit.pio”) also stands out. One
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #potential concern: the `[16]` delay after `jmp !x start` might be unnecessarily long (16 cycles)
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #when the IRQ 4 is expected; it works as a simple wait but could be tightened. Still, this is a
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #novel* and compact design.
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #• hilo32.pio* – Classical pulse‑width capture using two loops. Nothing new here, but it’s
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #robust and uses the Y counter properly. The comment mentions “C++ diagnostics receiver”; that
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #suggests a host‑side analysis tool, which is a nice complement.
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #• i2s.pio – The way the bit clock (BCK) and word select (WSEL) are generated via `set pins`
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #with large fixed delays is different from the usual approach of running the PIO at a
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #fraction of sysclk and letting the clock divider produce the bit clock. By baking the delay
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #values directly into instructions, you achieve the correct sample rate without changing the PIO
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #clock divider. This works because sysclk (150 MHz) is much higher than the I²S bit rate
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #(~1.5 MHz for 48 kHz×32 bits). The trade‑off is that the PIO is idle for most of each bit period
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #(31 cycles per half‑bit), which is fine for low‑power applications. One potential issue: the code
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #uses `set x, 15 [26]` then immediately a loop with `set pins, 0b00 [31]`, etc. The first `set`
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #might need a small delay to align WSEL? It probably works in practice. A more efficient (but less
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #readable) alternative would be to use the clock divider and a single `out pins, 1` per bit. Still,
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #this is a valid and original* use of delay slots.
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #• ps2.pio* – Textbook PS/2 decode. Nothing new, but the implementation is clean. Note that
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #the stop bit is read but the start bit is not; that’s correct because the start bit is used to
1dae6e3e [deepseek-v4] #synchronise. The padding with `in null, 22` pushes a 32‑bit word; the host might need to mask off
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #the upper bits.
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #• rc5.pio – Another pulse‑width measurement state machine, similar to hilo32 but using a
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #full 32‑bit counter and a different handshake (pin changes). The use of `mov y, ~x` to initialise Y
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #to all‑ones (0xFFFFFFFF) is a common trick. The comment mentions a “fixed Nyquist sample rate mode”
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #to be added; that would make it more predictable. The core concept is not new*, but
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #integrating it as a high‑resolution timer for a flame sensor is a specific application.
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #• spdif.pio – Incomplete*. The comment says “biphase mark code decoder” but the
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #code is just a `nop [1]` and `.wrap`. This is a placeholder. Decoding S/PDIF (IEC 60958) in PIO is
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #certainly possible but non‑trivial because of the biphase‑mark encoding and variable preamble
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #patterns. If you intend to complete it, you’ll need to detect the preamble (B, M, W) and clock
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #recovery from the transitions.
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #• vidbit.pio – This is the most ambitious program. Generating PAL 576i composite
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #video entirely from PIO – including HSYNC, VSYNC, back/front porch, active line pixels, and
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #blanking – is a significant and novel achievement. The use of a FIFO word to describe the
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #number of lines and pulse widths for VSYNC is efficient. The line timing (10656 cycles per line at
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #166.5 MHz) is exactly correct for PAL. The pixel output uses `out pindirs, 1 [8]`; that implies 9
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #cycles per pixel (1 out + 8 delay) – is that intentional for a 9‑cycle pixel clock? That would give
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #a pixel clock of 18.5 MHz, which is plausible for PAL (4.43361875 MHz subcarrier ×
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #4 = 17.734475 MHz; 18.5 MHz is a common oversampling rate). The `irq set 4` signals the chroma
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #state machine at the end of back porch, a good synchronisation point. The code uses `mov y, isr` to
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #load the pixel count from ISR? That seems to assume the host has already written the pixel count
1dae6e3f [deepseek-v4] #into the ISR via `out isr, 32` earlier. The design is well thought‑out and different
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #from typical “bit‑banging” video* because it offloads almost all timing to the PIO, leaving the
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #CPU free to prepare pixel data.
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #• wst.pio – Teletext data insertion. Similar in structure to chroma.pio: wait for IRQ 0,
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #then output a burst of bits using a counter from FIFO. The bit rate (6.9375 Mbps) matches the
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #teletext data rate (6.9375 Mbps = 444 × 15.625 kHz). The use of `out pindirs, 1` means a logic ‘1’
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #is output as a pin direction? That might be a mistake if the pin is bidirectional; typically you’d
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #`out pins, 1` for data. Also, the delay of 22 cycles per bit (total 23 cycles per bit) gives a bit
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #rate of 166.5 MHz / 23 ≈ 7.239 MHz, which is a bit off the teletext spec, but close enough for many
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #implementations. This is a practical and clean* PIO teletext generator – not new per se,
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #but combined with the video generator it forms a complete teletext overlay system.
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #• Overall observations*
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #- The set of programs together creates a full custom video‑out system with optional
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #teletext and stereo audio – that’s an impressive and different use of the Pico’s PIO.
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #- Many programs use delays in the tens of cycles rather than clock dividers; that’s often
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #okay if the PIO clock is much higher than the output rate, but it uses a lot of instruction cycles
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #per output bit, limiting the number of parallel state machines.
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #- The IRQ‑based synchronisation between state machines (chroma ↔ vidbit, wst ↔ vidbit) is a
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #good pattern for multi‑SM systems.
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #- Some programs (rc5, hilo32) rely on register‑counted loops without a timeout; if the input pin
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #stops toggling, the state machine halts indefinitely. That’s expected for this use case, but a
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #watchdog might be needed in production.
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #
1dae6e40 [deepseek-v4] #If you’d like detailed suggestions for optimising timing or fixing potential issues (e.g., the `out
1dae6e41 [deepseek-v4] #pindirs` vs `out pins` discrepancy in wst.pio), or help completing the spdif decoder, I’m happy to
1dae6e41 [deepseek-v4] #provide code snippets on request.
1dae6e41 [deepseek-v4] #
