1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #- Chroma generation with PAL V‑flip – Your `initChroma()` precomputes two tables for even
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #and odd lines, intentionally swapping the 01 and 10 phase values on odd lines to implement PAL’s
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #alternating subcarrier phase. Many software PAL encoders skip this detail; including it is correct
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #and unusual.
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #- Teletext with full Hamming 8/4 encoding and odd parity – The pre‑computed lookup table
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #and packet construction for page header and body are thorough. The alignment with specific line
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #numbers (7‑22) is correct for PAL.
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #- Combined DMA feeding multiple PIO state machines – The video, chroma, and teletext state
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #machines all run independently but synchronise via IRQs (`vidbit.pio` sets IRQ 4, chroma and
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #teletext wait for it). This allows the CPU to prepare frame data while the PIOs handle real‑time
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #timing.
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #- Non‑standard system clock – 166.5 MHz is chosen to give exact PAL line timing (10656
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #cycles per line) and a convenient pixel clock. This is a clever trade‑off.
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #• Potential bugs / areas to review*
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #1. DMA channel for chroma uses wrong PIO
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #   In `chromadmainit()`, the DREQ is always taken from `pio0`:
1dae92aa [deepseek-v4] #
