1db0f100 [deepseek-v4] #Thank you for sharing these files. The project is an ambitious RP2350‑based PAL video generator
1db0f100 [deepseek-v4] #combining composite sync/luma, colour subcarrier, teletext, audio, and PS/2 keyboard – all
1db0f100 [deepseek-v4] #synchronised via PIO state machines. I’ll comment on what is new or different and point out a few
1db0f100 [deepseek-v4] #technical points.
1db0f100 [deepseek-v4] #
1db0f100 [deepseek-v4] #Architectural highlights
1db0f100 [deepseek-v4] #
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #- Multi‑PIO design: video and teletext on `pio0`, chroma and audio on `pio1`, keyboard on
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #`pio2`. This isolates timing‑critical streams.
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #- Colour subcarrier via 2‑pin DAC: a simple 4‑level quadrature modulator using a pair of
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #GPIOs and a passive RC filter. This avoids external DACs and is a pragmatic trade‑off for
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #experimental colour.
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #- Synchronisation between vidbit and chroma using `irq 4` – a clean way to align the colour
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #burst to the back porch.
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #- Teletext insertion with Hamming 8/4 encoding and odd parity – a faithful (if minimal)
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #implementation of the World System Teletext standard, inserted in the vertical blanking interval.
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #- Continuous field update via DMA chaining (or immediate restart) – the main loop polls the
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #DMA completion and re‑triggers the next field without an explicit vsync interrupt. This is simple
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #but can work because the PIO stalls when its FIFO empties.
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #PIO program observations
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #`vidbit.pio`
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #- Timing for the front porch and back porch appears to have off‑by‑one errors in the constant
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #values (as noted in the earlier comment).
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #  - Back porch: `set y, 28` gives 28 iterations (29 actual cycles) → 900 cycles, but the
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #comment says 964.
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #  - Front porch: `set y, 7` gives 7 iterations → 226 cycles, comment says 258.
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #  The sum of the components would then be slightly off the 10656 target; check the exact line
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #timing after correction.
1db0f101 [deepseek-v4] #- The pixel data is output via `out pindirs,1` – this toggles the pin direction rather than the pin
1db0f102 [deepseek-v4] #level. If the pin is configured as open‑drain (with a pull‑up), this can produce a composite
1db0f102 [deepseek-v4] #signal. More typical is to use `out pins,1` and drive the pin directly. The comment says “bitmap =
1db0f102 [deepseek-v4] #[27 x 309/310]” but the PIO reads 32‑bit words and outputs one bit per pixel. That implies only two
1db0f102 [deepseek-v4] #luminance levels (black/white). For grey‑scale, multiple bits per pixel would be needed.
1db0f102 [deepseek-v4] #
1db0f102 [deepseek-v4] #`chroma.pio`
1db0f102 [deepseek-v4] #- The clock divider comment says `clkdiv=9.3885` (→ 17.734 MHz = 4‑× f_sc), but the C‑code uses
1db0f102 [deepseek-v4] #`2.3471f`, which yields ~70.96 MHz. This is a critical discrepancy: either the comment is
