1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #Furthermore, the `jmp !x start` is never executed after the first line because after `.wrap` the
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #program runs `start` which does `wait 1 irq 4` again? Actually the `.wrap` goes to the label
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #`start`. Yes, after `.wrap` it jumps back to `start`. So the program does wait for IRQ 4 on
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #every line. That is correct. The problem is that the DMA continues filling the FIFO while the PIO
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #is waiting, so when it finally waits and reads the counter, the FIFO may already contain leftover
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #data from the previous line.
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #Because the DMA runs continuously, the FIFO will be prefilled with data words. When the PIO waits
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #for IRQ 4, the FIFO already has words waiting. The first word it reads (`out x,32`) will be the
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #next word in the FIFO after the previous line’s data. That word is likely a data word, not
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #the intended counter. So every subsequent line gets a wrong counter.
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #Summary of necessary corrections
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #╭───────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #│  Aspect                   │  Current                 │  Required                                                                             │
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #│  Counter per line         │  2031                    │  959 (or 960)                                                                         │
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #│  Burst length             │  32 samples (2 words)    │  40 samples (2.5 words or pad)                                                        │
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #│  DMA synchronisation      │  Infinite loop, no sync  │  Restart DMA per line or use a ring that exactly matches one line’s FIFO consumption  │
1db17435 [deepseek-v4] #│  FIFO alignment           │  Counter word only once  │  Ensure first word after IRQ is counter; DMA must not pre‑fill during wait            │
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #╰───────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #A practical fix:
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #- Reduce `count` to 959 and adjust data buffer size accordingly.
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #- Use a single‑shot DMA triggered by the video IRQ (or by checking that chroma DMA is
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #busy). For each line, (re)start DMA with a transfer count of `1 + (samples/16)` words (where
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #samples = 960, so 1+60=61 words).
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #- Alternatively, keep the infinite ring but embed the counter word at the same offset every
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #line. That would require the ring buffer to contain multiple copies of the line structure, one
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #per line, with the per‑line IRQ still needed to ensure the PIO reads the correct word. This is more
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #complex.
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #Given the complexity, a simpler approach would be to rewrite the chroma PIO program to not use
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #a counter word but instead rely on a fixed‑length DMA transfer that exactly fills one line’s
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #worth of data. The PIO would loop for a fixed number of autopulls (e.g., 60 words) and then wait
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #for the next IRQ. This eliminates the need to count.
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #Would you like a corrected version of both the PIO program and the C++ code?
1db17436 [deepseek-v4] #
