1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #Code Review: vidbit – RP2350 PAL Video Signal Generator
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #Thank you for sharing these files. This is an impressive project that demonstrates a deep
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #understanding of PIO programming, DMA timing, and analog video signal generation on the RP2350. The
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #architecture is thoughtful – using separate PIO state machines for luma, chroma, and teletext, all
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #driven by DMA from pre-computed frame buffers. The interlace and progressive modes, optional
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #teletext (WST), and color subcarrier generation show careful attention to the PAL 576i standard.
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #Overall Strengths
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #- Modularity: Separate PIO programs for `vidbit`, `chroma`, and `wst` (though not shown)
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #allow independent control and potential synchronization via IRQ.
1e051349 [deepseek-v4] #- DMA-based pipeline: The main video state machine is fed by a DMA channel from a frame
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #buffer; chroma and teletext have their own DMA chains. This keeps CPU involvement minimal during
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #active video.
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #- Interlace support: The code properly handles even/odd fields by toggling frame buffers
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #(evenFrame/oddFrame) and adjusting line counts (309/310).
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #- Real-time frame drawing: The main loop redraws the frame via `drawFrame()` or
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #`drawFramei()` while the previous frame is being displayed. Double-buffering is implied.
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #- Peripheral integration: PS/2 keyboard, I2S audio, and UART boot-mode detection are
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #included, showing a system-level view.
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #Observations & Potential Issues
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #1. PIO Program `vidbit.pio`
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #- Cycle counts: The comments claim 10656 cycles per line. Let’s verify the main line loop:
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #  - HSYNC: `set y, HSYNCWAIT29` (26) + loop (26×2? Actually `jmp y--,hsync` with delay `[28]` →
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #2 cycles per iteration including the jmp) → Wait: need to calculate precisely. The `[28]` delay is
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #on the `jmp` instruction, not the `set`. That gives 2+28 = 30 cycles per iteration? But PIO `jmp`
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #with delay adds the delay cycles after the jump target is taken? Actually the delay slot applies to
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #the instruction itself. A `jmp` with a side-set/delay `[28]` means the jmp takes 1 cycle for the
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #jmp and then 28 cycles wait before the next instruction. So the loop is: `jmp y--,hsync [28]` –
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #each iteration takes 1 (jmp) + 28 (delay) = 29 cycles when condition true, and 1 cycle when
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #condition false (exit). The total for HSYNC wait is: `y` starts at `HSYNCWAIT29 = 26`, so 26
1e05134a [deepseek-v4] #loops true → 26×29 = 754 cycles, plus 1 cycle for the final jmp that falls through = 755. Then the
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #`jmp` after that? Actually there’s a `[12]` delay at the end of the line loop. Need to sum all
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #parts. It might be intentional, but double-check the total cycle count for a 166.5 MHz clock and
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #10656 cycles per line.
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #- `set pindirs, 0` before back porch: This sets the pin as input (high‑Z). But the DAC for
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #chroma is on separate pins; for the luma pin, setting it input will cause the pin to float unless
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #pull‑up/down is configured. You might want to set it to output with `0` value instead. In PAL, the
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #back porch is a blanking level (0 IRE), so output low is correct. Using `set pindirs, 0` makes the
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #pin high‑impedance, which may not be the intended voltage level.
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #- `out isr,32`: After the vsync pulse train, you read the ISR (input shift register) into
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #the output shift register? Actually `out isr,32` copies from OSR to ISR? Wait: `out` always takes
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #from the output shift register and puts it somewhere. `out isr,32` pulls 32 bits from OSR and
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #writes to ISR. If you want to load the line counter from FIFO, you should do `out x, 32` before the
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #line loop. But you already did `out x,32` for vsync. In the line loop, you use `mov y, isr` to get
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #the pixel count. That assumes ISR contains the pixel count. But ISR is normally used for input. You
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #might be using ISR as temporary storage, but it’s not initialized at the start of each line. The
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #`out isr,32` after vsync seems to load ISR from OSR? Actually after vsync, OSR holds the line
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #counter from FIFO? The code does `out x,32` a few times; after vsync, `out x,32` reads the next
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #FIFO word into x (line counter), then `out isr,32` would overwrite ISR with whatever is in OSR
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #(which was the vsync pulse widths?). That looks like a bug.
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134b [deepseek-v4] #- Delay values: Many delays are `[28]` or `[31]` or `[12]`. These must be within the
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #allowed range (0-31). They are. But ensure the total cycle count per line matches the PAL line
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #length (64 µs = 10656 cycles at 166.5 MHz). You might want to add a comment with the calculation.
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #- Pixel output: The active line loop uses `out pindirs, 1 [8]`. This outputs 1 bit from OSR
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #to the pin direction? That sets the pin direction to output for a 1 bit, input for a 0 bit? That is
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #unconventional. Usually you output data via `out pins, ...` not `out pindirs`. Perhaps you are
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #using the pin direction as a digital output where 1 = high and 0 = low? But then you would need to
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #set the pin low for blanking. The code sets `pindirs, 1` during HSYNC and `pindirs, 0` during back
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #porch – this toggles the pin between output and input, not between high and low. To output a video
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #waveform, you should use `set pins, ...` or `out pins, ...`. I suspect this is a conceptual
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #mistake: `pindirs` controls whether the pin is an output (1) or input (0). If you set it to output
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #with `pindirs, 1` and the pin itself is left low (maybe default), you get a low level. If you set
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #it to input, the pin floats. The correct way is to keep the pin always an output and modulate its
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #value via `out pins, ...`. Consider redesigning the PIO program to output data bits on the pin.
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #2. PIO Program `chroma.pio`
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #- Clock divider: Comment says `clkdiv=9.3885` but code uses `2.3471f`. Which is correct?
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #9.3885 would give about 17.73 MHz (166.5/9.3885 = 17.73), which matches 4× f_sc (4.43361875 MHz).
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #2.3471 would give 166.5/2.3471 ≈ 70.96 MHz, which is too fast. Probably a typo in the code (should
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #be ~9.388).
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #- `out pins,2 [2]`: Outputs 2 bits to the two pins (CHROMA100, CHROMA330). Good.
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134c [deepseek-v4] #- Loop structure: It uses `wait 1 irq 4` to synchronize with the video field. The video PIO
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #does not issue IRQ 4 in the current `vidbit.pio`. There’s a commented out `irq set 0`. That might
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #be intentional for future use. As is, the chroma DMA may never start because IRQ 4 is never set.
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #Consider adding an `irq` instruction in the video PIO at the start of each line or field.
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #- FIFO join: `piosmsetfifojoin(pio, sm, PIOFIFOJOIN_TX)` joins both
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #FIFOs into a 8‑word TX FIFO. That’s fine for high‑bandwidth chroma data.
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #3. C++ Code (vidbit.cpp)
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #- DMA ring buffer for chroma: The DMA channel uses
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #`dmachannelconfigsetring` with size 9 (512 byte boundary). That’s clever for
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #wrapping around `colorWord` buffer, but note that the buffer size is `colorWords2` (256 words)
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #with a ring power of 9 (512 bytes = 128 words). That mismatch will cause the wrap to happen at 128
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #words, but the buffer is 256 words; the ring only aligns to 512 bytes. Actually `set_ring(true, 9)`
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #means the read address wraps at a 512-byte boundary (9 bits → 2^9 = 512 bytes = 128 words). The
1e05134d [deepseek-v4] #buffer is 256 words, so after 128 words it will wrap back to the start. That’s intended if you want
