1e052c08 [claude-opus-4-7] #A few things to think through before testing:
1e052c08 [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e052c08 [claude-opus-4-7] #• 1. All four SMs share the same instruction memory* — `pioaddprogram` is called
1e052c08 [claude-opus-4-7] #once, all SMs reference `colorfifo_offset`. Good — saves three quarters of the program slots.
1e052c08 [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e052c08 [claude-opus-4-7] #• 2. Palette duplication* — each SM has its own TX FIFO, so the same 4-entry palette must
1e052c08 [claude-opus-4-7] #be pushed into each of the four FIFOs separately. That's what `loadColorPalette` per-SM in the loop
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #does. Worth noting: if you ever want different palettes per SM (e.g. odd/even line variants), you
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #have that flexibility for free.
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #• 3. PIO block choice* — pio0 has vidbit+wst, pio1 has chroma+audio (i2s), pio2 has
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #keyboard. So pio2 has 3 SMs free, not enough for 4. You'll either need to consolidate (move ps2 to
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #pio1, or chroma+audio share pio0 instead) or split colorfifos across PIO blocks. Splitting is fine
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #but `pioaddprogram` and pin init must run per PIO block used.
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #• 4. Wait pin polarity — `jmp pin stall` jumps while pin is high, so colorpump must
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #pulse the wait line low* to release the SM. Your colorpump.pio's `set pins, 14` (= 0b1110)
1e052c09 [claude-opus-4-7] #pulls bit 0 low — that matches if SM0's wait pin is the LSB of colorpump's `set` group. The mapping
