1e0542eb [claude-opus-4-7] #C++14 onwards supports the `0b` prefix for binary literals directly, and you can use `'` as a digit
1e0542eb [claude-opus-4-7] #separator (C++14) to group bits visually:
1e0542eb [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #uint32_t colors[] = {
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #	0b00'110'011'001'111'10000,
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #	0b00'000'000'000'000'00000,
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #	// ...
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #};
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #A few things worth knowing:
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #• Type and width* — `0b...` literals are typed as `int` by default (or `unsigned int` if
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #too large for signed). For a `uint32_t` initialiser the compiler will convert, but for explicitness
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #in tables you can suffix:
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #0b0011'0011'0011'1110'0000u	// unsigned
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #0b0011'0011'0011'1110'0000ul	// unsigned long
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #0b0011'0011'0011'1110'0000U	// same as u
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #• Width is not implied by leading zeros* — `0b0011` and `0b11` are the same value (3). The
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #visible bit-width is purely cosmetic; only the stored value matters. So if you want a packed 18-bit
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #colorfifo word in a 32-bit slot, write the full 18 bits with leading zeros for readability but
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #understand the top 14 bits of the uint32 are still zero.
1e0542ec [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #• Separator placement is free* — `'` can go anywhere between digits and doesn't affect
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #value:
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #0b000'111'000'111'000'111	// group by 3 (per cycle, 3 channels)
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #0b111000'111000'111000	// or by per-cycle triplets the other way
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #For your colorfifo palette, grouping by 3 (one group = one cycle of Y/Pb/Pr) reads naturally
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #right-to-left in time since bit 0 (rightmost in the literal) comes out of the OSR first. If
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #you want left-to-right-in-time in source, you'd write the bits reversed — which fights against how
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #everyone reads binary numbers. Probably worth keeping binary literals as "rightmost cycle is
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #earliest" and just commenting accordingly, or using the array-of-cycle-bytes form from before where
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #source order = time order naturally.
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #• MSVC quirk* — older MSVC needed `/std:c++14` or later; pico-sdk's GCC has supported `0b`
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #since forever.
1e0542ed [claude-opus-4-7] #
