1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #That is actually a brilliant way to leverage the RP2350's new indexed FIFO reads. You are
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #effectively building a 16-entry lookup table by concatenating the 4 FIFOs across the 4 state
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #machines.
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #Here is exactly how your architecture achieves this:
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• 1. The 4-Bit Color Split (The Pump)*
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #When you feed a 4-bit color index (0 to 15) into the `colorpump` SM, it splits that nibble in half:
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• `out pins, 2` takes the lower 2 bits and puts them on the shared `COLORI0` and `COLORI1`
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #pins.
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• `out x, 2` takes the upper 2 bits and puts them in the `X` scratch register.
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• 2. The Trigger Routing*
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #The `colorpump` then uses those upper 2 bits (`X`) to decide which of the 4 trigger pins to pulse
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #low:
1e05d690 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• If X=0 (Colors 0-3): `set pins, 14` (Binary `1110`) -> Pulls GP2 low.
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• If X=1 (Colors 4-7): `set pins, 13` (Binary `1101`) -> Pulls GP3 low.
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• If X=2 (Colors 8-11): `set pins, 11` (Binary `1011`) -> Pulls GP4 low.
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• If X=3 (Colors 12-15): `set pins, 7`  (Binary `0111`) -> Pulls GP5 low.
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• 3. The FIFO Lookup (The Fifos)*
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #Because each of the four `colorfifo` SMs is configured to wait on a different trigger pin (`jmp pin
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #stall`), only one SM wakes up.
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• That awakened SM reads the lower 2 bits from the shared pins (`mov y, pins`).
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• It executes `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`.
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• Because it's configured with `.fifo txget`, the FIFO acts like a static 4-word RAM. It grabs the
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #specific pulse train for that color, shifts it out to the video pins, and goes back to sleep.
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #How to set this up in C++
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #To make this static 16-color palette work, your initialization routine just needs to preload the 16
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #pulse trains into the 4 SMs before you enable them.
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #You would do this right inside your `colorpump_init` (or `initColorfifos`) function. Conceptually,
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #it looks like this:
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #// Assuming color_trains[16] holds your 18-bit pindirs sequences
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #int color_index = 0;
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d691 [gemini-3.1-pro] #for (int sm = 0; sm < 4; sm++) {
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #	// 1. Configure the SM (pin mappings, etc.)
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #	// ...
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #	// 2. Preload the 4 colors for this specific SM
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #	for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #		pio_sm_put_blocking(pio2, sm, color_trains[color_index]);
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #		color_index++;
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #	}
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #	// 3. Enable the SM
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #	pio_sm_set_enabled(pio2, sm, true);
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #}
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #Once you push those 4 words into each SM's TX FIFO, they sit there permanently. The `txget` mode
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #ensures they are not consumed when read by `rxfifo[y]`.
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #• One crucial detail for your SM setup:*
1e05d692 [gemini-3.1-pro] #When configuring the 4 `colorfifo` SMs, you need to assign a different `jmp` pin to each one so
