1e072971 [gemini-3.1-pro] #To answer your question directly: No, you cannot jump directly on a bit in the FIFO. The
1e072971 [gemini-3.1-pro] #PIO hardware requires you to shift the bits into a register (`X`, `Y`, or `PC`) before you can
1e072971 [gemini-3.1-pro] #branch on them.
1e072971 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e072971 [gemini-3.1-pro] #Because you cannot chain `jmp x--` (since it always decrements X, which breaks the
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #cascade), the standard and safest way to decode those 2 bits in PIO is a binary tree. You use `jmp
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #!x` (which checks for zero without modifying X) and `jmp x--` (to subtract 1 and move to
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #the next check).
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #Because the different branches take a different number of cycles to reach, we pad the shorter paths
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #with `nop` so that every single color takes exactly 10 cycles to execute.
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #Here is the correct, position-independent, perfectly cycle-balanced code for your pump:
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #; colorpump.pio
1e072972 [gemini-3.1-pro] #; outs 2 gpio pins for color index
