1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #The relay project is new to me in our current conversation. I've reviewed the files thoroughly.
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #Here are my observations on what stands out as notable or different from typical projects:
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #What's distinct
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #1. Slopmark timestamp format – hexadecimal encoding of sixteenths of a second since
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #2025.4.12 is unconventional but consistent. It appears throughout the logging system.
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #2. Four‑stage naming evolution – the README documents a clear lineage: roha → foundry →
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #forge → fountain. This suggests iterative rewrites rather than incremental patches.
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #3. Multiple model provider support – OpenAI, DeepSeek, Google (Gemini), Anthropic are all
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #supported with native SDKs. The `connectAccount()` dispatcher cleanly handles each API's
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #idiosyncrasies (e.g., Anthropic file uploads via `files-api-2025-04-14`, Google's `functionCall`
1e4283ab [deepseek-v4] #format).
