1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #Thank you for sharing the vidbit project files. This is entirely new content compared to the relay
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #sharing earlier. I can see you're working on an RP2350-based PAL video signal generator with some
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #advanced features: composite video (via luma+chroma resistor DAC), component video (Y/Pb/Pr), and
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #World System Teletext insertion, all driven by PIO state machines and DMA.
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #A few observations on what looks different or notable:
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #1. colorpump / colorfifo – This seems to be a novel approach to color rendering: a
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #two-stage PIO pipeline where `colorpump` selects a color index (0–3) from pixel data and triggers
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #the corresponding `colorfifo` machine, which outputs a pre-calculated 6-cycle pulse train for Y,
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #Pb, Pr. The `makecolortrain` function uses PDM-like patterns to produce six levels per
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #channel. That's a clever way to generate analog color without a high-speed DAC.
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #2. 576i vs 288p – The code includes both interlaced (LACE) and progressive paths. The
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #`colorpump` DMA transfers 27 words × 4 × 305 lines for interlaced, suggesting each line has 864
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #pixels (27 words × 32 bits/word / 2 = 432? Actually 27×32 = 864 bits, so 864 pixels per line). That
1e429e25 [deepseek-v4] #matches PAL standard timing.
1e429e26 [deepseek-v4] #
1e429e26 [deepseek-v4] #3. Teletext – The WST (World System Teletext) implementation uses Hamming 8/4 encoding and
1e429e26 [deepseek-v4] #odd parity, inserted on lines 6–22 and 318–335. The body/header functions build 48-byte packets.
1e429e26 [deepseek-v4] #The DMA updates teletext data each field with a rolling row counter.
1e429e26 [deepseek-v4] #
1e429e26 [deepseek-v4] #4. Audio – There's an `i2sinit` call and a commented `STREAMAUDIO` section,
