1e9412e1 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e1 [deepseek-v4] #Yes, another `out x,32` consumes one word (the line counter), then `out isr,32` consumes another
1e9412e1 [deepseek-v4] #(or sets ISR from OSR → another pull). Then the `line:` loop begins, which per line does:
1e9412e1 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e1 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e1 [deepseek-v4] #mov y, isr   ; (no pull)
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #active:
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #    out pindirs, 1   ; (autopull after 32 bits? Actually each out shifts 1 bit, so autopull after 32 pixels)
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #    ...
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #    jmp y--, active [8]
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #And before that, at the top of the line loop:
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #set pins,0
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #set pindirs,1
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #...
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #These do NOT consume FIFO words. The only FIFO consumption per line is inside the `active` loop:
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #each `out pindirs, 1` shifts 1 bit, and after 32 such shifts, autopull fetches the next word. So
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #each line consumes 864 bits = 27 words, exactly as expected.
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #Therefore the total expected FIFO sequence for a full frame (in interlaced mode with 309 lines for
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #even field or 310 for odd) is:
1e9412e2 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #[vsync count] [32 pulse widths] [line count] [pixel count] [27 words × N lines]
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #And for progressive mode, N should be 625 lines (for 625‑line PAL progressive). The pixel count
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #should be 863, and line count should be the number of lines per frame (624? Actually the line
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #counter is decremented each line; it starts at N‑1 where N is number of lines in that field/frame).
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #What likely goes wrong in progressive mode
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #Your `initFrame()` (not shown, but presumably in `pal576i.cpp` or similar) likely builds a buffer
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #that matches the interlaced field structure (even/odd fields with different line counts) but not a
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #full progressive frame. For progressive, you need 625 lines of 27 words each, plus the
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #preamble words. If the buffer is shorter or the preamble is missing, the PIO will try to `PULL`
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #beyond the buffer, the DMA will complete its transfer early, and the PIO will stall on an empty
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #FIFO.
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #Additionally, the `progressiveWordCount` variable must be set to the total number of words for the
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #progressive frame (including preamble). If it's wrong, the DMA will either stop too early or run
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #off the end of the buffer.
1e9412e3 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #How to fix
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #1. Verify the progressive frame buffer construction – Ensure `initFrame()` writes exactly:
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #   - 1 word for vsync count (31)
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #   - 32 words for pulse widths (typically all 31? But you need to check the PAL sync pulse widths)
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #   - 1 word for line counter (for progressive: 624? i.e., 625 lines - 1)
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #   - 1 word for pixel counter (863)
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #   - 625 × 27 = 16875 words for pixel data
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #   Total `progressiveWordCount` = 1 + 32 + 1 + 1 + 16875 = 16910 words.
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #   (Adjust if your blanking lines include more or fewer lines; make sure the line count and number
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #of pixel data lines match.)
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #2. Check that the pixel data for vertical blanking lines is present – The PIO will consume
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #27 words for every line in the line loop, even during vertical blanking (lines after the active
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #video and before the next field). Your buffer must supply 27 words per line for all 625 lines.
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #3. Correct the `vidbit.pio` porch timing – The earlier timing analysis showed that the line
1e9412e4 [deepseek-v4] #loop is 120 cycles short (total 10536 rather than 10656). This mis‑timing will cause the PIO to run
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #through the line loop faster, consuming pixel data at a higher rate than the DMA is configured for
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #(since each line now takes fewer cycles, the pixel consumption rate increases). That will deplete
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #the FIFO faster and cause TX stalls. Fix the `HSYNCWAIT29`, `BACKPORCHWAIT32`, and
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #`FRONTPORCH_WAIT32` as previously recommended, and adjust the final JMP delay to match the correct
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #line length.
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #4. After fixing the line timing, recalculate the pulse width delays – The vsync pulse
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #widths were chosen assuming 10656 cycles per line. If you change the line timing, the vsync pulse
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #lengths (in cycles) must be recalculated to maintain correct PAL vertical sync (5 equalising pulses
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #+ 5 broad pulses + 5 equalising pulses). Currently you compute them based on 31965 cycles total?
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #The code comment says `total 31965 - 2*32` – verify this corresponds to the vsync period of 192 µs.
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
1e9412e5 [deepseek-v4] #
