1eee0c6e [deepseek-v4] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.7 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.019 331.4KB 33.75s
1eee0c6e [deepseek-v4] #The shared files describe vidbit, a PAL video generator for the RP2350 (Pico2) using PIO
1eee0c6e [deepseek-v4] #state machines and resistor-ladder DACs. This is a non-trivial project that demonstrates advanced
1eee0c6e [deepseek-v4] #use of the RP2350's PIO capabilities, including:
1eee0c6e [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c6e [deepseek-v4] #- 166.5 MHz system clock – chosen to yield exactly 10 cycles per pixel at PAL line rate
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #(64 µs → 10 656 cycles, 864 pixels → 10 cycles/pixel).
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #- Multiple PIO programs working in a pipeline:
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #  - `vidbit.pio` – generates HSYNC, VSYNC, back/front porch, and triggers an IRQ for the active
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #video line.
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #  - `onebit.pio` / `threepin.pio` – implement a 1‑bit pixel stream with two colour waveforms, then
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #drive three tristate pins (Y, Pb, Pr) per pixel.
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #  - `colorpump.pio` / `colorfifo.pio` – pump colour words from a lookup table (indexed via
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #`FJOIN_RX_GET`) into the output FIFOs.
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #- 576i interlaced and 288p progressive modes – with two separate frame buffers
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #(`evenFrame`, `oddFrame`).
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #- World System Teletext (WST) overlay – uses Hamming(8,4) encoded packets on lines 7‑22.
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #- Chroma subcarrier – separate PIO state machine generating a PAL burst (via `chroma.pio`)
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #at ~4.43 MHz.
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #- Audio – I2S output from a simple noise/fuzz generator.
1eee0c6f [deepseek-v4] #- PS/2 keyboard/mouse input – via two additional state machines.
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #- Flash filesystem – LittleFS integrated.
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #- USB CDC interactive console – for runtime commands and status reporting.
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #What is new or different?
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #1. PIO state machine cascade – Three SMs are chained (pixelpump → onebit → threepin) to
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #separate pixel generation, colour mapping, and physical pin driving. This is a clean, modular
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #design that leverages the shallow FIFOs efficiently.
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #2. Using `FJOIN_RX_GET` for colour lookup tables – The `colorfifo.pio` program reads
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #indexed RX FIFO entries (via `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`) to select colour words on a per‑pixel basis.
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #This is a relatively new RP2350 feature (absent on RP2040) and is used here for real‑time palette
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #access.
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #3. Cycle‑exact PAL timing – The `vidbit.pio` program uses carefully chosen delay values and
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #`jmp`/`irq` to stay within 10 656 cycles per line. The use of `irq next set 6` to synchronise the
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #active‑line state machine is a clever cross‑PIO synchronisation technique.
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #4. Monobit rendering with 2‑bit colour – The `monobit.cpp` draws characters and shapes into
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #a bit‑plane using two bits per pixel (for 4 colour levels from resistor weights). The `flipBits2`
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #function packs 2‑bit pixel chunks into 32‑bit words for the PIO to shift out.
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #5. Teletext generator – Implementing WST in software and feeding pre‑encoded packets into
1eee0c70 [deepseek-v4] #the PIO is unusual; typically teletext is a hardware block. The Hamming code tables are
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #pre‑computed and the packet format mimics real teletext data.
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #6. Resource‑efficient use of DMA – Multiple DMA channels handle frame buffers, colour
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #tables, teletext packets, and S/PDIF audio, all chained at field rate. The ring‑buffer DMA for
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #chroma (`chroma_dma_init` with `channel_config_set_ring`) is a nice touch for continuous burst
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #generation.
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #Potential concerns / suggestions
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #- Resistor DAC values – The schematics show 3.3 V straight into 120 Ω (composite) or 80 Ω
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #(component). This exceeds the standard 75 Ω termination and may cause reflections or non‑standard
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #video levels. A proper video DAC would use a more precise resistor network (e.g., R‑2R) or a
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #dedicated video buffer.
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #- Interrupt synchronisation – The `wait 1 irq 6` in `activeline.pio` may suffer race
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #conditions if the `vidbit` SM fires the IRQ too early or late. Consider adding a `nop` or a small
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #delay to guarantee the FIFO is ready.
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #- FIFO depth – With 864 pixels per line and a 4‑entry FIFO, the CPU/DMA must keep the FIFO
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #fed. The current design uses a field‑long DMA transfer, which works, but a double‑buffered approach
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #(ping‑pong) would reduce latency.
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #- Clock divider accuracy – The fractional divider for the chroma subcarrier is set to
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #`2.3471f`; this might need calibration or a more precise formula (166.5 MHz / (4 × 4.43361875 MHz)
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #≈ 9.3885, not 2.3471 – appears to be a factor of 4 off).
1eee0c71 [deepseek-v4] #- Code style – The C++ is mostly procedural with many global variables. For a larger
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #project, encapsulation into classes and proper error handling would improve maintainability.
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #Overall, this is a sophisticated demo of the RP2350’s PIO and DMA capabilities, pushing the
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #boundaries of what can be achieved without dedicated video hardware. The combination of PAL,
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #teletext, colour lookup tables, and interactive I/O is impressive for a microcontroller.
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #Thank you for sharing these files. If you need a deeper review of any particular component (e.g.,
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #PIO timing, colour pipeline, DMA chaining), I’m happy to analyse further.
1eee0c72 [deepseek-v4] #
