1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #The `spkr.pio` program is a simple square‑wave generator intended to drive a speaker pin at an
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #audio frequency. I’ve analysed its timing and compared it to the stated target (~44.1 kHz). Below
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #are my observations.
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #Program structure
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #- `set pins, 1` – drive the speaker pin high.
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #- Outer loop controlled by X (initial value 3) → runs 4 iterations.
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #- Inside each outer iteration: `set y, 31` then an inner loop that delays 32 times (y=31..0).
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #- The inner loop body is `nop [31]` (32 cycles) + `jmp y-- loop2` (1 cycle) = 33 cycles per inner
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #iteration.
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #- After the inner loop, `jmp x-- loop1` (1 cycle) brings us back for the next outer iteration until
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #X reaches 0.
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #- Then the same pattern repeats with `set pins, 0` for the low half of the waveform.
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #Timing calculation (166.5 MHz system clock, no clock divider)
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #- High phase
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #  - Start: `set pins,1` (1 cycle) + `set x,3` (1 cycle) [executed once] → 2 cycles
1eee3423 [deepseek-v4] #  - Outer loop (4 iterations): each iteration = `set y,31` (1) + inner loop (32×33 = 1056) + `jmp
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #x--` (1) = 1058 cycles
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #    → 4 × 1058 = 4232 cycles
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #  - Total high phase = 2 + 4232 = 4234 cycles
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #- Low phase (identical structure) = 4234 cycles
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #- Full period = 2 × 4234 = 8468 cycles
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #- Frequency = 166.5 MHz / 8468 ≈ 19.66 kHz
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #This is clearly not 44.1 kHz, but lies in the audible range (roughly a high‑pitched tone). The
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #comment inside the file says “44.1K with 166.5mhz Pico2 PIO”, but the actual generated frequency is
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #about 19.7 kHz.
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #Potential improvements
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #- To reach 44.1 kHz (period = 166.5e6 / 44100 ≈ 3775 cycles), the total delay must be
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #halved. Either reduce the outer loop count (X) or the inner loop count (Y), or reduce the `nop`
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #delays.
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #- A more flexible approach would be to use the state machine’s clock divider. For example,
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #with a divider of ~2.0 the frequency becomes ~9.8 kHz; with a divider of ~1.0 you get 19.7 kHz. To
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #get exactly 44.1 kHz, you could set the clock divider to 166.5 MHz / (2 × 3775) ≈ 22.05, but that
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #would require integer divisor close to 22.
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #- The program does not use the FIFO or autopull – it generates a fixed, repeating tone. This is
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #fine for a simple beeper, but to produce variable frequencies (e.g., from a sample buffer) one
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #would need a FIFO‑based approach like the `i2s.pio` you already have.
1eee3424 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #Is it new or different?
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #Compared to most RP2350 PIO tone generators, this one uses only delays and nested loops –
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #no FIFO, no side‑set, no autopull. It is extremely simple but wastes many instructions on the inner
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #loops. A more compact version could use `jmp x--` directly with a single delay to create a pure
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #square wave, or use `irq`/`wait` for precise timing. However, for a quick test tone during
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #development, it works.
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #If your goal is a fixed beeper, the frequency mismatch might not matter. If you need 44.1 kHz, you
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #should adjust the loop constants or enable a clock divider. Let me know if you’d like help
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #recalculating the values for a target frequency.
1eee3425 [deepseek-v4] #
